# PROSPECTIVE summary scaffold, generated by scripts/new-provenance-summary.sh.
# TODO: replace the placeholder summaryText/decisionPoint below with the
# actual distilled reasoning behind this PR before committing — see
# docs/plans/TRANSCRIPT_SUMMARY_GUIDELINES.md. Timestamps below are real
# (from `git log main..HEAD` on branch feat/185-llm-chat-scoping); everything else that
# isn't marked TODO can be left as-is.

ghpull:624 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "Scope frontend LLM chat: architecture, auth, safety boundaries" ;
    bl:number 624 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:185 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr624 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:185 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-30T09:44:12Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-30T09:47:56Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr624Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #185 asked for a design decision, not code: where the LLM provider key lives and who calls it, how much the chat agent is allowed to do (draft vs. tool-calling), what safety rails a future tool-calling loop needs, whether to abstract over providers now, and how much dataset context to send. Rather than surveying options neutrally, the task was to ground a concrete recommendation in what this repo already does. Checked docs/plans/AUTH.md (the Read/Write/Admin permission model and #164's body-inspecting classify() fix), the NetworkPolicy/SSRF-hardening trio (#120, #137, #138), and confirmed frontend.html/deploy/ ship no Content-Security-Policy today. That last fact is what tips the recommendation: with no CSP, a browser-direct fetch() to the provider (Option A) needs literally zero new server code, while a server-proxied call (Option B) would mean adding an async HTTP client to sparql_endpoint and either reusing or duplicating the SSRF-hardening machinery NetworkPolicy already exists for -- a machinery built specifically because outbound fetches in this codebase are treated as a real attack surface. Combined with draft-only scope (agent proposes text, user clicks Run through the existing query box), every drafted query a user actually executes already flows through auth_middleware/classify()/--read-only/the owl:Nothing 409 check with no new permission code needed -- #164 in particular is why an LLM-drafted mutation disguised as a 'query' still gets classified Write correctly. Landed on: browser-direct calls, draft-only first, Claude-only (no premature provider abstraction), and VoID/Service-Description-based context (existing endpoints) instead of a raw-triple dump, with a one-time localStorage-gated privacy notice mirroring the PR #255 pattern. Wrote docs/plans/LLM_CHAT_ARCHITECTURE_PLAN.md with per-question recommendations and rationale, linked it from FRONTEND_PLAN.md, and filed #626/#628/#629 as sub-issues of epic #184 (via the GitHub sub_issues REST API, since `gh issue create --project` errors on this repo's project due to the Projects-classic deprecation) at Status Todo for the user to review." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:624 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr624Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr624 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr624Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Recommended Option A (browser calls the LLM provider directly with the user's own key) over Option B (dagalog server proxies the call), because frontend.html/deploy/ currently set no Content-Security-Policy, so Option A needs zero new server surface, while Option B would require adding an async outbound HTTP client to sparql_endpoint plus either reusing or reimplementing the SSRF hardening NetworkPolicy already exists for." ;
    agp:alternative "Option B (server-side proxy) was rejected for the first cut: it would let the server enforce outbound policy centrally, but at the cost of a new async HTTP dependency in sparql_endpoint, the key transiting and potentially being logged server-side, and duplicating or extending NetworkPolicy's SSRF hardening (#120/#137/#138) for a single fixed destination -- disproportionate for what a fetch() from the browser already achieves with no new code." .
